import{_ as o,o as t,c as a,ag as s}from"./chunks/framework.D1z9wg1y.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Guard hooks","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"safeguards/guard-hooks.md","filePath":"safeguards/guard-hooks.md"}'),d={name:"safeguards/guard-hooks.md"};function c(n,e,h,r,i,l){return t(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[s('<h1 id="guard-hooks" tabindex="-1">Guard hooks <a class="header-anchor" href="#guard-hooks" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Guard hooks&quot;">​</a></h1><p>A permission rule matches the text of a tool call against a pattern. Some mistakes need more than a pattern: the parsed command, the working directory, the state of a worktree, or the form of a token. A guard hook is a program that reads the tool call before it runs and answers with code. It refuses the call with a reason that tells the agent what to do instead, asks you, or lets the call run. So a guard catches a mistake and also teaches the next command. Like the permission lists, a guard prevents accidents and is no boundary (<a href="./security-model">The security model</a>). <a href="./git-safety">Git safety</a> groups the guards of <code>git</code> and <code>gh</code> by the step of the work.</p><p>A hook is a program that the frontend runs at a fixed event, for example before a tool call or at the start of a session. The frontend sends the event as JSON on standard input. A <code>PreToolUse</code> hook runs before a tool call and can refuse it: exit status 2 refuses the call and shows the hook&#39;s standard error to the model, and an <code>ask</code> decision on standard output shows a prompt to the user. The hooks of this page use the Claude Code events <code>PreToolUse</code>, <code>PostToolUse</code>, <code>SessionStart</code>, <code>UserPromptSubmit</code>, <code>PermissionRequest</code>, <code>WorktreeCreate</code> and <code>WorktreeRemove</code>.</p><p>The sources are in <code>hooks/</code>. <code>harness install --apply</code> copies them to <code>~/.claude/hooks/</code>. Claude Code calls them through the <code>hooks</code> section of its settings, which <code>harness install --apply</code> writes from <code>settings/settings.proposal.json</code>. On the <code>Bash</code> matcher, the directory and worktree tools and the Kaimon tools, Claude Code runs one program, the guard package <code>hooks/guard/</code>, which holds every check of a tool call. Antigravity runs the same package, with <code>--from antigravity</code>, on every tool call. oh-my-pi runs it with <code>--from omp</code> through its guard extension <code>adapters/omp/guards.ts</code>, and OpenCode with <code>--from opencode</code> through its plugin <code>adapters/opencode/plugins/guards.ts</code>, on every tool call. Each of the three frontends runs its own copy of the package, which <code>harness install</code> writes below <code>hooks/guard/</code> in its directory: <code>~/.gemini/config/hooks/guard/</code>, <code>~/.omp/agent/hooks/guard/</code> and <code>~/.config/opencode/hooks/guard/</code>. Each hook still has one source. <code>hooks/probe.py</code> holds the test cases of the hooks, and <code>harness test</code> runs it.</p><p><code>harness install --apply</code> writes the command of each Python hook, in <code>~/.claude/settings.json</code> and in Antigravity&#39;s <code>hooks.json</code>, as the absolute path of the interpreter of the harness&#39;s Python environment, then the hook&#39;s path, each one shell word, quoted where the path holds a space. So a hook runs on the environment&#39;s Python, not on the <code>python3</code> that comes first on the <code>PATH</code>. Each Python hook keeps the shebang <code>#!/usr/bin/env python3</code>, for a run by hand such as the examples on this page. When the environment is missing, the command of a hook fails to start, with exit status 127. Claude Code reads an exit status other than 2 as a hook error that does not refuse the call, so under Claude Code such a hook fails open while the environment is missing. The guard&#39;s command ends in <code>|| exit 2</code>, so a guard that cannot start refuses the call. Antigravity reads a handler that prints nothing as a block.</p><p>The hooks prevent accidents. They are not a security boundary: each one reads the text of a command. <a href="./security-model">The security model</a> says what they stop and what they do not stop. The page groups the hooks: first the guard package and its checks, then <code>edit-scope.py</code>, then <code>worktree.py</code> and the session hooks, and last the probe.</p><h2 id="the-guard-package" tabindex="-1">The guard package <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-guard-package" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The guard package&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The guard is one program in <code>hooks/guard/</code> that decides a tool call before it runs. Claude Code runs it as a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook on the matchers <code>Bash</code>, <code>mcp__ccd_directory__.*|EnterWorktree|ExitWorktree</code>, <code>mcp__kaimon__.*</code> and <code>WebFetch|WebSearch</code>, with the command <code>&lt;python&gt; ~/.claude/hooks/guard --from claude || exit 2</code>. The <code>Bash</code> matcher runs <code>rtk hook claude</code> first, then the guard. Antigravity runs its copy as a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook on every tool call, with the command <code>&lt;python&gt; ~/.gemini/config/hooks/guard --from antigravity</code>; <a href="./../components/adapters">the adapters page</a> describes its answers. The plugins of oh-my-pi and OpenCode run <code>&lt;python&gt; &lt;their directory&gt;/../hooks/guard --from omp</code> or <code>--from opencode</code> on every tool call, with the interpreter that <code>guard-paths.json</code> beside them names under <code>python</code>; they send the event as JSON and apply the answer <code>{&quot;block&quot;: …, &quot;reason&quot;: …}</code>.</p><p>A table for each frontend, <code>frontends/claude.py</code>, <code>frontends/antigravity.py</code>, <code>frontends/omp.py</code> and <code>frontends/opencode.py</code>, turns the payload into a neutral call: the action, the command, the paths, the paths below which the call reads, the working directory and the caller. The tables of Antigravity, oh-my-pi and OpenCode also run the path-list check <code>path_list.py</code>, which reads the frontend&#39;s <code>guard-paths.json</code>; Antigravity&#39;s table runs the lift check <code>sandbox_lift.py</code> on a shell call that it does not deny, which lifts a command out of the sandbox only where the line matches a closed grammar and Claude Code&#39;s sandbox rule lifts it. One module per check reads that call: the staging, write, sandbox, <code>gh api</code>, <code>gh pr create</code>, <code>gh</code> watcher, rm, archive, git write, push deletion, web, token, Kaimon and directory checks below. Each check gives <code>deny</code>, <code>ask</code>, <code>allow</code> or <code>pass</code> with a reason. The guard answers the first of <code>deny</code>, <code>ask</code>, <code>allow</code> and <code>pass</code> that a check gives, with that check&#39;s reason, in the form of the frontend: for Claude Code exit 2 with the reason on standard error for <code>deny</code>, a JSON decision for <code>ask</code> and <code>allow</code>, and no output for <code>pass</code>. A tool that the table does not name passes. oh-my-pi and OpenCode cannot ask the user from a plugin, so their tables answer an <code>ask</code> as a block whose reason tells the model to ask the user in chat; <code>plugin.py</code> holds what the two tables share: the reading of the message, the size check of the command and the answer.</p><p>The directory check asks before a session leaves <code>~/Research</code>, or removes a worktree. A <code>change_directory</code> or <code>request_directory</code> call of the Claude desktop app passes when the path resolves inside <code>~/Research</code>, after <code>~</code>, <code>..</code> and symbolic links. An <code>EnterWorktree</code> call with a <code>path</code> that resolves inside <code>~/Research</code> passes only when the path is in a repository&#39;s <code>.claude/worktrees/</code>; the check refuses any other such path. Claude Code asks the person for every <code>EnterWorktree</code> path outside <code>.claude/worktrees/</code>, as a safety check that neither a hook&#39;s <code>allow</code> nor auto mode approves. The worktrees here are in <code>~/Research/.worktrees/</code>. The refusal names the call that needs no prompt: <code>cd &lt;path&gt;</code> as a Bash call of its own. An <code>EnterWorktree</code> call with only a <code>name</code> passes, because <code>worktree.py</code> then decides the place. An <code>ExitWorktree</code> call passes only with <code>action: &quot;keep&quot;</code> and no <code>discard_changes</code>. Every other such call gets an <code>ask</code> decision.</p><p>The guard fails closed. It refuses a payload that is not UTF-8 or not a JSON object, or that has no <code>tool_name</code>; for a tool that it checks, a <code>tool_input</code> that is no object, a <code>Bash</code> call without a command, and a command of more than 1 MiB. A check that must resolve a path against the working directory refuses the command when the payload names none. A guard that raises refuses the call with the error. The checks read a command without <code>shlex</code>: a <code>#</code> inside a word, a backslash-newline and a quoted operator are read as the shell reads them, and a quoted operator or a joined line is also read the other way, so that neither hides a part of the command. <code>tests/guard/corpus.jsonl</code> holds the decision of each payload that the probe checks, and each difference from the base&#39;s hook scripts and plugin checks, with its reason. A check of a shell command reads each path through <code>hooks/guard/paths.py</code>, which reads each directory and name once in each mode and asks each directory once whether it holds a <code>.git</code>; every check and every reading takes the walk of a word from one memo; the write check asks git once per work tree. A probe case finds no file-system read elsewhere in the package but a few named ones, which read once per process or one path of a directory call. The probe counts the reads of ten commands of 1 MiB (a long <code>cd</code> word, many <code>rm</code> operands, many <code>git init</code> outside a work tree), each at most two or three reads per distinct name of the command plus a constant, and the guard decides each of its timed commands of 1 MiB within 10 s. Test it from <code>hooks/</code> with the environment&#39;s interpreter, which is Python 3.14; the guard exits with status 2 on a <code>deny</code>:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;tool_name&quot;:&quot;Bash&quot;,&quot;tool_input&quot;:{&quot;command&quot;:&quot;git add -nA&quot;},&quot;cwd&quot;:&quot;/&quot;}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ~/.local/share/research-harness/python/bin/python</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> guard</span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;"> --from</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> claude</span></span></code></pre></div><h3 id="the-staging-check" tabindex="-1">The staging check <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-staging-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The staging check&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>no_blind_stage.py</code> refuses a <code>git add</code> that stages more than the paths it names, on each shell call of every frontend.</p><p>It reads the command, splits it into segments, and finds each <code>git add</code>. It refuses <code>-A</code>, <code>-u</code>, <code>--all</code>, <code>--update</code>, <code>--no-ignore-removal</code>, a short-flag cluster that holds <code>A</code> or <code>u</code>, and the pathspecs <code>.</code>, <code>./</code> and <code>:/</code>. The flag cluster is the reason for the check: a deny glob cannot match <code>git add -nA</code> without also matching a file name that holds an <code>A</code>. The refusal tells the model to stage its paths by name.</p><h3 id="the-write-check" tabindex="-1">The write check <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-write-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The write check&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>no_shell_file_write.py</code> refuses a shell command that writes a file in a git working tree, on each shell call of every frontend.</p><p>It refuses a redirection such as <code>&gt;</code> or <code>&gt;&gt;</code>, <code>tee</code>, an in-place flag cluster of <code>sed</code>, <code>perl</code> or <code>awk</code>, and a <code>python3 -c</code> or a Python heredoc that opens a file to write. It resolves each target against the working directory and each <code>cd</code> of the command. A target under a scratch directory passes: <code>$TMPDIR</code>, <code>/tmp/</code>, <code>/private/tmp/</code>, <code>/var/folders/</code>, <code>/dev/</code> or <code>~/Research/.scratch/</code>. In a git working tree, every file is refused unless git ignores it. Outside one, only a file with a source suffix such as <code>.jl</code> or <code>.md</code> is refused. A quoted <code>&gt;</code> is text, not a redirection. The refusal tells the model to use the <code>Edit</code> and <code>Write</code> tools. It does not see a target that the shell computes, a Julia one-liner, or a writer inside a string argument such as <code>perl -e</code>.</p><h3 id="the-sandbox-check" tabindex="-1">The sandbox check <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-sandbox-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The sandbox check&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>sandbox_semantics.py</code> refuses a command whose shape makes the Claude Code sandbox give a false result. Claude Code runs the whole check. Antigravity, oh-my-pi and OpenCode run its working-tree check alone on each shell call, and Antigravity&#39;s lift check reads the same rule to decide whether a command runs inside or outside the terminal sandbox.</p><p>The settings let a command run outside the sandbox only when every part of it starts with <code>git</code>, <code>gh</code>, <code>glab</code> or <code>ps</code> and no part redirects a file. A sandboxed <code>gh</code> cannot reach the network, and a sandboxed <code>git</code> cannot write <code>.git</code>. The failure then looks like a negative result or a certificate error. The check refuses four shapes:</p><ul><li>a <code>gh</code>, <code>glab</code> or <code>git</code> write inside <code>$( … )</code>, backticks or a loop;</li><li>a top-level <code>gh</code>, <code>glab</code> or <code>git</code> write in a command that is not exempt, for example after <code>cd other &amp;&amp;</code>, or a <code>gh</code> command that holds a backtick or <code>$(</code>;</li><li>a <code>$?</code> in a command that runs sandboxed;</li><li>a path argument of <code>git init</code>, <code>git clone</code>, <code>git worktree add</code>, <code>git worktree move</code> or <code>git bundle create</code> that starts with <code>/</code>, <code>~</code> or <code>$</code>, or holds <code>..</code>.</li></ul><p>It also refuses a <code>git init</code> or <code>git clone</code> whose target is inside a git working tree: the working-tree check. It follows each <code>cd</code> of the command to find the target. Under Claude Code a <code>cd</code> outside the agent&#39;s working directories is reset before the next call, so such a command runs in the agent&#39;s root and leaves a repository in another working tree. Each refusal gives the form that works, for example a separate Bash call.</p><h3 id="the-gh-api-check" tabindex="-1">The <code>gh api</code> check <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-gh-api-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The `gh api` check&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>gh_api_writes.py</code> asks before a <code>gh api</code> write that is not a review or a comment in an own organisation, and refuses a remote delete, on each shell call of every frontend.</p><p>It finds each top-level <code>gh api</code> and <code>glab api</code> call and determines the HTTP method in the same way as <code>gh</code>: <code>-X</code> or <code>--method</code>; otherwise POST when a body flag is present; otherwise GET. A GET, a HEAD and a <code>search/…</code> endpoint pass. It refuses a DELETE from <code>gh api</code> or <code>glab api</code>. Any other <code>glab api</code> call passes. A review, an inline comment, a reply, and a comment on a pull request, an issue or a commit pass in a repository of an own organisation, so the review agents can post there. The endpoint must be <code>repos/&lt;owner&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;/…</code> with an <code>&lt;owner&gt;</code> of the profile&#39;s <code>org</code> list, compared without case, and the call must name no <code>--hostname</code>; the check does not read <code>GH_HOST</code>, so a host that the environment names is beyond its reach. The same write to any other repository asks, because an agent can post a secret in a comment on any public repository. It also asks when the check cannot read the repository: a placeholder such as <code>{owner}</code>, an owner or a repository name with a character that GitHub does not allow in it, such as a <code>%</code>, and a repository <code>.</code> or <code>..</code>. The check reads the endpoint as <code>gh</code> gets it, without percent-decoding. A GraphQL query passes when it holds no mutation, or when each mutation is a review or comment operation, in any repository: its node IDs name no repository that the check can read. Every other write gets an <code>ask</code> decision, and the user sees a prompt. An edit of an existing comment also asks.</p><p>The check reads a GraphQL query where <code>gh</code> reads it: the text of <code>-f query=…</code>, which <code>gh</code> sends as it is; the file of <code>-F query=@&lt;path&gt;</code>; and the string <code>query</code> of the JSON object in the file of <code>--input &lt;path&gt;</code>. A relative path resolves against the call&#39;s working directory. A query that the check cannot read asks: a relative path in a call with no working directory, or in a command with a <code>cd</code>, <code>pushd</code> or <code>popd</code>; a file whose name a word of another part of the command holds, because that part can write the file before <code>gh</code> reads it, as <code>cp other.graphql q.graphql &amp;&amp; gh api graphql -F query=@q.graphql</code> would; standard input (<code>-</code>); a path that names no regular file; a file that is not UTF-8; an <code>--input</code> that is no JSON object with a string <code>query</code>; and a call with more than one query. So write the query file in a call of its own. The check reads the file before <code>gh</code> runs, so a file that another process changes in between is not the file that it decided on.</p><p>A Git Data write in one of the user&#39;s own organisations also passes: a POST to <code>repos/&lt;owner&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;/git/blobs</code>, <code>git/trees</code>, <code>git/commits</code> or <code>git/refs</code>. These calls make a new branch, as a <code>git push</code> of a new branch does. The <code>git/refs</code> endpoint only creates a ref. A PATCH of <code>git/refs/&lt;ref&gt;</code> can move a branch by force, so it still asks. The own organisations are the <code>org</code> list of the profile: the file that <code>RESEARCH_HARNESS_PROFILE</code> names, else <code>~/.config/research-harness/profile.toml</code>. The check compares the owner without case, and reads the profile once per process. A call with <code>--hostname</code> asks. The check fails closed: when the profile is missing, cannot be read, is not UTF-8 TOML, or has no <code>org</code> list of strings, the write asks. Every frontend runs the guard with the user&#39;s <code>HOME</code> and environment, so each one finds the same profile.</p><h3 id="the-gh-pr-create-check" tabindex="-1">The <code>gh pr create</code> check <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-gh-pr-create-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The `gh pr create` check&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>gh_pr_create.py</code> decides a <code>gh pr create</code> by the repository that it names, on each shell call of every frontend. It finds each top-level <code>gh pr create</code> call and each call of its alias <code>gh pr new</code>, also after assignments such as <code>GH_REPO=…</code>, and reads every value of <code>-R</code> and <code>--repo</code>, in the forms <code>-R x</code>, <code>-Rx</code>, <code>-R=x</code>, <code>--repo x</code> and <code>--repo=x</code>, also at the end of a cluster of one-letter flags such as <code>-dR x</code>. The value of another flag that takes one, such as <code>--title</code>, is not a flag.</p><p>A call with no <code>-R</code> or <code>--repo</code> is refused: <code>gh</code> then opens the pull request in the repository that the checkout or <code>GH_REPO</code> names, which the command does not show. The reason tells the agent to add <code>--repo &lt;owner&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;</code>. A call with a value that is not <code>&lt;owner&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;</code> of an own organisation asks: another owner, a value with a host, and any value when the profile gives no own organisation. The check reads every value, not only the last one that <code>gh</code> takes, so a second <code>--repo</code> cannot send the call elsewhere. A call whose every value names an own organisation passes, so the <code>allow</code> rules let it run: <code>gh pr create -R &lt;org&gt;/*</code> and <code>--repo &lt;org&gt;/*</code>, first or after other arguments. No <code>allow</code> rule names the alias <code>gh pr new</code>, so Claude Code asks for it. The own organisations are those of the <code>gh api</code> check.</p><p>Under oh-my-pi, no pattern of <code>harness.yml</code> prompts for <code>gh pr create</code>, so the call runs under <code>yolo</code> also inside a pipe or after <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, and the guard extension refuses it or blocks it with the reason to ask the user.</p><h3 id="the-gh-watcher-check" tabindex="-1">The <code>gh</code> watcher check <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-gh-watcher-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The `gh` watcher check&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>gh_watch_interval.py</code> refuses a <code>gh</code> watcher that polls the GitHub API faster than once every 30 seconds, on each shell call of every frontend. A watcher is a top-level <code>gh run watch</code> call or a <code>gh pr checks</code> call with <code>--watch</code>, also after assignments such as <code>GH_REPO=…</code>. The check reads the last value of <code>-i</code> or <code>--interval</code>, in the forms <code>-i 60</code>, <code>-i60</code>, <code>-i=60</code>, <code>--interval 60</code> and <code>--interval=60</code>, because <code>gh</code> takes the last one.</p><p>A watcher with no interval is refused: <code>gh run watch</code> then refreshes every 3 seconds and <code>gh pr checks --watch</code> every 10 seconds. A watcher whose interval is not a whole number of at least 30 seconds is refused too. The reason tells the agent to add <code>--interval 60</code>. Each refresh makes several API calls, and all sessions and agents of one account share one rate limit. A watcher on a run of half an hour at the default interval makes thousands of calls and trips GitHub&#39;s secondary rate limit; after that, the REST Actions endpoints answer 403 for every session of the account until the limit lifts. <code>git ls-remote</code> and <code>git fetch</code> do not use the API, so they still read a branch, a tag or a <code>gh-pages</code> deploy.</p><h3 id="the-rm-check" tabindex="-1">The rm check <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-rm-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The rm check&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>rm_scope.py</code> refuses a recursive <code>rm</code> outside the scratch zones, and asks before a <code>git rm</code> or a <code>git merge</code> outside a worktree, on each shell call of every frontend.</p><p>It follows the working directory through each <code>cd</code> and resolves each operand. A recursive <code>rm</code> passes when every operand is strictly below a worktree, <code>~/Research/.worktrees/&lt;name&gt;/</code>, or strictly below <code>~/Research/.scratch/</code>. It refuses a recursive <code>rm</code> of <code>/</code>, of the parent directory of the home directories, <code>/Users</code> or <code>/home</code>, or of any path below it. So a worktree itself goes through <code>git worktree remove</code>. A plain <code>rm</code>, and an operand that it cannot resolve, get no decision. A <code>git rm</code> gets an <code>ask</code> decision outside a worktree, with <code>-f</code> or <code>--force</code>, or with <code>-C</code>, <code>--git-dir</code> or <code>--work-tree</code>. A <code>git merge</code> in a worktree passes: the merge is local, and <code>git reset --hard ORIG_HEAD</code> undoes it. Outside a worktree, or with <code>-C</code>, <code>--git-dir</code> or <code>--work-tree</code>, it gets <code>ask</code>, except a <code>--ff-only</code> or <code>--no-edit</code> merge with none of <code>--no-ff</code>, <code>--squash</code>, <code>--no-commit</code> and <code>-m</code>. So the settings allow <code>git merge *</code>, and keep an <code>ask</code> only for <code>--abort</code>. A command that holds <code>rm</code> or <code>merge</code> and that it cannot read gets <code>ask</code>.</p><h3 id="the-archive-check" tabindex="-1">The archive check <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-archive-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The archive check&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>git_archive_output.py</code> refuses a <code>git archive</code> that writes its file with <code>-o</code> or <code>--output</code>, on each shell call of every frontend, and names the commands that read the tree of a commit without a permission prompt.</p><p>An agent writes the archive to read the unmodified tree of a commit, usually the base of its branch. <code>git</code> runs outside the sandbox, so <code>-o</code> can write to any path. For this reason the settings have <code>ask</code> rules on <code>-o</code>, and the user gets a prompt for what is only a read. The check refuses the call, and its message names other commands. <code>scripts/base-tree.jl</code> puts the tree, or some paths of it, into a new directory. <code>git show &lt;rev&gt;:&lt;path&gt;</code> gives one file, and <code>git ls-tree -r --name-only &lt;rev&gt;</code> gives the list of files. A tar file that is itself the result goes to the user.</p><p>It finds each top-level <code>git … archive</code> call, after global options such as <code>-C</code>, and reads the option in each spelling that git takes: <code>-o &lt;file&gt;</code>, <code>-o&lt;file&gt;</code>, behind other short options as in <code>-vo &lt;file&gt;</code>, <code>--output</code>, <code>--output=&lt;file&gt;</code>, and any prefix of <code>--output</code>. An argument after <code>--</code> is a path. A <code>git archive</code> to stdout passes.</p><h3 id="the-git-write-check" tabindex="-1">The git write check <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-git-write-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The git write check&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>git_writes.py</code> allows three <code>git</code> writes when the repository shows that nothing is lost, and asks otherwise, on each shell call of every frontend. A permission rule cannot make this decision: it does not see the working directory or the state of a worktree. A hook&#39;s <code>allow</code> does not lift a matching <code>ask</code> or <code>deny</code> rule in Claude Code, so the settings have no rule for these forms, and the check answers both ways.</p><ul><li><code>git worktree remove --force &lt;path&gt;</code>, or <code>-f</code> once: allow when the path is below <code>~/Research/.worktrees/</code>, a remote-tracking ref contains the worktree&#39;s HEAD, and no tracked file is modified or staged. Untracked and ignored files can go. Otherwise ask, with a reason that names the commit or the files that the removal loses.</li><li><code>git cherry-pick</code>: allow in a worktree below <code>~/Research/.worktrees/</code>, or in a checkout whose <code>origin</code> is a GitHub repository of an own organisation. Otherwise ask.</li><li><code>git push --force-with-lease origin &lt;branch&gt;</code>, or with <code>HEAD:refs/heads/&lt;branch&gt;</code>: allow when <code>origin</code> is in an own organisation and the lease names no ref or the pushed branch. Otherwise ask. In the <code>&lt;branch&gt;</code> form, the branch must be a local branch, and <code>origin</code> must have no <code>remote.origin.push</code> refspec, which can map the branch onto another remote branch.</li><li>A lease push that names <code>main</code>, <code>master</code> or the branch that <code>origin/HEAD</code> names as its target is refused, also inside a command of several parts such as <code>git fetch &amp;&amp; git push --force-with-lease origin main</code>, after another write in the same part, as in a <code>find</code> with two <code>-exec</code>, and inside the script of <code>sh -c</code>, <code>bash -c</code> or <code>eval</code>. The check reads the target of each argument after <code>push</code>, the value of an option aside: <code>&lt;branch&gt;</code>, <code>+&lt;branch&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;src&gt;:&lt;branch&gt;</code>, each also with <code>refs/heads/</code> or <code>heads/</code>, which <code>git</code> reads as <code>refs/heads/</code>. It reads <code>origin/HEAD</code> in the working directory of the call and in the repository that the push works on: the directory after each <code>cd</code> before it and each <code>-C</code> or <code>--git-dir</code> of its <code>git</code>. An option that the shell expands into a lease, such as <code>--force-$w</code>, counts as a lease. A lease push that names no branch can reach a default one, so it is refused too: no refspec after the remote, as with <code>--all</code> or <code>--mirror</code>, or a refspec whose target is <code>HEAD</code>, <code>@</code> or empty, or that holds a glob, a brace, <code>$</code> or a backtick. The value of an option such as <code>-o</code>, <code>-vo</code> or <code>--recurse-submodules</code> is no refspec.</li></ul><p>The check allows only a command that is one <code>git</code> call, with no operator, no <code>cd</code> and no option before the subcommand. It finds the subcommand after the global options of <code>git</code>. Any other form of these writes asks, for example <code>-C</code>, <code>-f</code> twice or a lease after the remote. A <code>worktree remove</code> with any option counts as forced, because <code>git</code> reads <code>-ff</code> and a prefix of <code>--force</code> as force. A plain <code>--force</code> push gets no decision from the check; the <code>deny</code> rules of the settings refuse it. The check reads the state with <code>git</code> in the working directory of the call, with <code>core.fsmonitor</code> off. When <code>git</code> fails or cannot run, the call asks. The own organisations are the <code>org</code> list of the profile, as the <code>gh api</code> check reads it.</p><h3 id="the-push-deletion-check" tabindex="-1">The push deletion check <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-push-deletion-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The push deletion check&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>git_push_delete.py</code> asks before a <code>git push</code> whose refspec has an empty source, on each shell call of every frontend. A refspec <code>:&lt;ref&gt;</code>, also <code>+:&lt;ref&gt;</code>, deletes <code>&lt;ref&gt;</code> on the remote, and a lone <code>:</code> pushes every matching branch. The check finds each <code>git</code> call of each part of the command, after the global options of <code>git</code> such as <code>-C</code>, also behind another command as in <code>find -exec</code> and inside the script of <code>sh -c</code>, <code>bash -c</code> or <code>eval</code>. It reads each argument after the remote that is neither an option nor the value of one, as the git write check does. A command that holds <code>push</code> and that the check cannot read gets <code>ask</code>.</p><p>A permission rule cannot state this in Claude Code without a warning at each start: the rule needs a literal <code>:</code> and then a wildcard, and Claude Code reports every <code>:*</code> that is not at the end of a rule. The <code>ask</code> rules of the settings keep <code>--delete</code>, <code>-d</code> and <code>--prune</code>.</p><h3 id="the-web-check" tabindex="-1">The web check <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-web-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The web check&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>web_identity.py</code> refuses a web request that identifies the user, on each web call and each shell call of every frontend. The web calls are Claude Code&#39;s <code>WebFetch</code> and <code>WebSearch</code>, OpenCode&#39;s <code>webfetch</code> and <code>websearch</code>, oh-my-pi&#39;s <code>web_search</code> and its <code>read</code> of an <code>http</code> or <code>https</code> URL, and Antigravity&#39;s <code>read_url_content</code> and <code>search_web</code>. The check reads each text percent-decoded and without case. It refuses:</p><ul><li>a web call any of whose string arguments holds the query parameter <code>mailto=</code>, <code>email=</code>, <code>e-mail=</code>, <code>api_key=</code>, <code>api-key=</code>, <code>apikey=</code> or <code>access_token=</code> after <code>?</code> or <code>&amp;</code>, or holds the user&#39;s email address;</li><li>a shell command that holds such a parameter, or holds <code>://</code> and the user&#39;s email address.</li></ul><p>A literature index such as OpenAlex or Crossref asks automated clients for an email address or a key, and a fetched page can ask an agent to create a key with the user&#39;s email. The reason tells the agent to send the request anonymously and to report an index that rate-limits it as a failed source. A placeholder address is refused too, because the parameter is refused whatever its value. A parameter name in prose or in a pattern passes, such as <code>grep &#39;mailto=&#39;</code>, and so does a search for an author by name. The user&#39;s email address is <code>git config --get user.email</code>, read at most once per process; without git or without the key, the check reads the parameters alone. A request that a script builds from a file, and a request of a browser or an MCP tool, are beyond its reach.</p><h3 id="the-token-check" tabindex="-1">The token check <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-token-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The token check&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>token_forms.py</code> refuses a call that holds a token of a known form, on each shell call, each web call and each Kaimon call of every frontend: a shell command, or a string argument of a web or Kaimon call, that holds one of these forms.</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left;">form</th><th style="text-align:left;">pattern</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="text-align:left;">GitHub classic and app</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>gh[pousr]_</code> and 36 or more of <code>[A-Za-z0-9]</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">GitHub fine-grained</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>github_pat_</code> and 22 or more of <code>[A-Za-z0-9_]</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">GitLab</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>glpat-</code> and 20 or more of <code>[A-Za-z0-9_-]</code></td></tr><tr><td style="text-align:left;">Anthropic, OpenAI and providers of the same form</td><td style="text-align:left;"><code>sk-</code> and 20 or more of <code>[A-Za-z0-9_-]</code></td></tr></tbody></table><p>The check reads each text as it is and percent-decoded, with the case kept, and a prefix counts only where no ASCII letter, digit, <code>_</code> or <code>-</code> stands right before it. A prefix alone, a shorter body, an upper-case prefix, a word such as <code>task-…</code> and a reference such as <code>$GH_TOKEN</code> pass. The reason names the form, and holds neither the token nor the command, so the token check&#39;s refusal does not show the token again. The reason tells the agent to name the token by its variable. The token check stands first in each list of checks that holds it, before the path list too, and the guard answers the first refusal; so its reason answers every call in which it finds a token, also where another check refuses the call, such as the web check on a parameter <code>access_token=</code> or the <code>gh api</code> check on a DELETE.</p><p>The check prevents an accident and is no boundary. A token that is encoded in another way, split into parts, given without its prefix, or read by a script from a file passes it, and so does a form that the table does not name. The forms are those that GitHub, GitLab and the <code>sk-</code> providers publish.</p><h3 id="the-kaimon-check" tabindex="-1">The Kaimon check <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-kaimon-check" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The Kaimon check&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>kaimon_session.py</code> decides every Kaimon call: Claude Code&#39;s <code>mcp__kaimon__&lt;tool&gt;</code>, Antigravity&#39;s <code>call_mcp_tool</code> calls to <code>kaimon</code>, oh-my-pi&#39;s <code>mcp__kaimon_&lt;tool&gt;</code> and OpenCode&#39;s <code>kaimon_&lt;tool&gt;</code>.</p><p>Each Kaimon tool takes its session key under one name, or under none: <code>ex</code> takes <code>ses</code>, the tools that address a session, such as <code>manage_repl</code>, <code>type_info</code> and <code>run_tests</code>, take <code>session</code>, and the others, such as <code>check_eval</code>, <code>ping</code> and <code>start_session</code>, take neither. Kaimon ignores a parameter that a tool does not take and then uses the session that the agent is bound to, or otherwise the only one connected, which can be another agent&#39;s. So the check refuses three calls:</p><ul><li>a key under a name that the tool does not take, such as <code>session</code> on <code>ex</code> or <code>ses</code> on <code>manage_repl</code>; the message names the tool&#39;s own key;</li><li>an <code>ex</code> without <code>ses</code>, and a <code>manage_repl</code> without <code>session</code>;</li><li>a key that names no live session.</li></ul><p>A session is live when its gate&#39;s file <code>&lt;cache&gt;/sock/&lt;session id&gt;.json</code> exists and the process of its <code>pid</code> runs; the key is a prefix of the session id. The cache is <code>$KAIMON_CACHE_DIR</code>, else <code>$XDG_CACHE_HOME/kaimon</code>, else <code>~/.cache/kaimon</code>, as KaimonGate resolves it. Kaimon removes the file when the session shuts down or when it reaps a gone one, and a process that dies first leaves it behind. Kaimon finds a gate only through its file, so a key without one names no session that Kaimon routes. The socket files are no evidence, since Kaimon leaves them behind when a session ends. The check reads the files at each call and keeps nothing. It passes when it cannot tell: no directory, a file that cannot be read or holds no integer <code>pid</code>, a remote gate&#39;s key <code>tcp-…</code>. Under WSL, Kaimon also finds the gates of the Windows side, through files in a directory that the check does not read, so the check refuses their keys. <code>manage_repl</code> is not checked for a live session, because its <code>shutdown</code> removes a session whose process has gone. A tool that no table of the check names passes. The refusal of a missing key, like that of a dead key, names the live sessions, sorted and each once, or says that none is live; when the directory cannot be listed, it names none. The messages name the rule of <code>skills/build-part/evidence.md</code>.</p><h3 id="the-path-list-and-oh-my-pi-s-own-rows" tabindex="-1">The path list and oh-my-pi&#39;s own rows <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-path-list-and-oh-my-pi-s-own-rows" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The path list and oh-my-pi&#39;s own rows&quot;">​</a></h3><p><code>path_list.py</code> refuses a call that reaches a path of the frontend&#39;s <code>guard-paths.json</code>: for Antigravity, oh-my-pi and OpenCode, whose own permission rules bind only their file tools. Every path of a call and every word of a shell command meets the <code>deny</code> list; a write or an edit meets the <code>edit</code> list, which refuses, and then the <code>ask</code> list, which asks. OpenCode&#39;s table adds the list in <code>$OPENCODE_CONFIG_DIR/guard-paths.json</code>, whose <code>allow</code> refuses each home path and each absolute path below a directory of user data outside it, and refuses an in-place shell edit such as <code>sed -i</code> with a short message. oh-my-pi&#39;s table adds its own directory, <code>~/.omp</code>, as a <code>deny</code> rule but for the copies of the skills, the rules, the instructions and <code>RTK.md</code>, and as an <code>edit</code> rule whole; reads the paths of a hashline or patch header; and refuses a sub-agent&#39;s write to <code>agent://</code>, which wakes a parked agent. Each table reads a missing or unusable list as a refusal of every call. It is a text check: a path that a command builds at run time passes it.</p><h2 id="edit-scope-py" tabindex="-1"><code>edit-scope.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#edit-scope-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`edit-scope.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook asks once per worktree and session before an edit of a git hook or a CI workflow: a path below <code>.githooks/</code> or <code>.github/workflows/</code> under <code>~/Research</code>. A git hook runs outside the sandbox at the next commit, and a workflow runs in CI with the repository&#39;s secrets when its branch is pushed. An <code>ask</code> on each such edit stops protecting anything once a person approves forty of them in a row.</p><p>Claude Code runs it on the <code>Edit</code>, <code>Write</code>, <code>MultiEdit</code> and <code>NotebookEdit</code> tools twice. As a <code>PreToolUse</code> hook it gets <code>ask</code> for such a path in a main checkout, and for one below a worktree, <code>~/Research/.worktrees/&lt;name&gt;/</code>, unless this session holds a grant for that worktree. As a <code>PostToolUse</code> hook, <code>edit-scope.py --record</code>, it writes the grant after an edit there ran: only an approved edit runs, so only an approval makes a grant. The grants are in <code>~/.claude/logs/edit-scope.jsonl</code>, which the sandbox and an <code>Edit</code> deny keep from a session. The settings hold no <code>Edit</code> ask for these paths, because an <code>ask</code> rule outranks the hook&#39;s silence. OpenCode, oh-my-pi and Antigravity run no such hook and ask on each edit: their path lists take the two paths from <code>EDIT_SCOPE_ASKS</code> of <code>lib/harness/frontends.py</code>.</p><p>It fails closed to a prompt on a call that it cannot read. A hook that does not run lets every such edit through, because the settings hold no rule for them. Test it:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;session_id&quot;:&quot;s&quot;,&quot;tool_input&quot;:{&quot;file_path&quot;:&quot;&#39;&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">$HOME</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;&#39;/Research/Harness/.githooks/pre-push&quot;}}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ./edit-scope.py</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="worktree-py" tabindex="-1"><code>worktree.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#worktree-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`worktree.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook creates and removes the worktrees of Claude Code. Claude Code runs it on the events <code>WorktreeCreate</code> and <code>WorktreeRemove</code>. The reason is the sandbox: a <code>git worktree add</code> to a path below <code>~/Research/.worktrees/</code> runs sandboxed, where it cannot write <code>.git</code>.</p><p>On <code>WorktreeCreate</code> it fetches <code>origin</code> in the repository that holds <code>cwd</code>. It then creates <code>~/Research/.worktrees/&lt;Repository&gt;-&lt;slug&gt;</code> on the branch <code>name</code>, from <code>origin/HEAD</code>, or from <code>origin/main</code> if <code>origin/HEAD</code> is not set. It prints the path on standard output. An existing branch is checked out, and an existing worktree of the repository is printed as it is. A name <code>agent-&lt;hex&gt;</code>, from a sub-agent, gets a detached worktree with no branch. The hook copies the main checkout&#39;s <code>Manifest.toml</code> into the new worktree when the manifest holds every package of the worktree&#39;s <code>[deps]</code> at a version inside its <code>[compat]</code>. Otherwise it copies none and says why on standard error, because Julia does not check <code>[compat]</code> at <code>using</code>, and a stale manifest loads old dependencies. On <code>WorktreeRemove</code> it runs <code>git worktree remove</code> without <code>--force</code>, so git refuses a worktree with changes, and the branch stays.</p><p>It fails closed: on each error it exits with status 1 and gives the reason, and it creates no worktree. Test it:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;hook_event_name&quot;:&quot;WorktreeCreate&quot;,&quot;cwd&quot;:&quot;/&quot;,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;x&quot;}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ./worktree.py</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> ; </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &quot;exit </span><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">$?</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="install-drift-py" tabindex="-1"><code>install-drift.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#install-drift-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`install-drift.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook warns when <code>~/.claude/</code> is behind the sources that <code>harness install</code> installs. Claude Code and Antigravity run it on the event <code>SessionStart</code>.</p><p><code>harness install --apply</code> writes the stamp <code>~/.claude/.harness-install.json</code>. The stamp holds the sources and a SHA-256 digest over the installed paths, their modes and the source bytes. The hook computes the same digest from the sources and compares the two. It warns when they differ, when the stamp is missing or cannot be read, and when a source cannot be read. The warning goes to the user and to the session, and it names the command to run. It does not run <code>harness install</code>, and an edit of the model tables does not count as drift.</p><p>It only warns: it blocks nothing and exits with status 0 for each input. <a href="./../daily-use#the-drift-warning-at-session-start">Typical use</a> says what to do when the warning appears.</p><h2 id="prompt-log-py" tabindex="-1"><code>prompt-log.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#prompt-log-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`prompt-log.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook writes one JSON line for each permission dialog to <code>~/.claude/logs/permission-requests.jsonl</code>. The environment variable <code>LOG</code> names another file. Claude Code runs it on the event <code>PermissionRequest</code>, for every tool, just before it shows the dialog.</p><p>Each line holds the time, the session, the working directory, the permission mode, the tool and its input. It cuts each string of the input to 400 characters, so a <code>Write</code> call does not log the whole file. The log shows which prompts a person still answers, by tool, by command and by mode. The transcript does not show this.</p><p>It decides nothing and prints nothing, so the dialog appears as it does without the hook. Every code path exits with status 0, also when the hook cannot read the input or write the log. Test it:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;tool_name&quot;:&quot;Bash&quot;,&quot;tool_input&quot;:{&quot;command&quot;:&quot;ls&quot;}}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> LOG</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;">=</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">$TMPDIR</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">/p.jsonl</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ./prompt-log.py</span></span></code></pre></div><h2 id="kaimon-note-py" tabindex="-1"><code>kaimon-note.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#kaimon-note-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`kaimon-note.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook adds a note to a <code>Bash</code> call that runs a Julia probe in a fresh <code>julia</code> process. Such a probe pays the start of the process and the load of its packages on every call; a warm Kaimon session pays them once. Claude Code runs the hook on <code>PreToolUse</code> for <code>Bash</code>, in one entry with <code>rtk hook claude</code> and the guard. It runs the three in parallel, so the hook reads the command as Claude Code sends it, and a refusal of the guard wins over the note.</p><p>A simple command of the call is a probe when its program, after the reserved words such as <code>then</code>, <code>do</code> and <code>!</code> and after the environment assignments, is <code>julia</code> and it runs code: with <code>-e</code>, <code>-E</code>, <code>--eval</code> or <code>--print</code>, with a first argument that ends in <code>.jl</code>, or with code on its standard input from a pipe or a redirection, also one into a group <code>( … )</code> or <code>{ …; }</code> that holds it. The value of an option, such as the <code>no</code> of <code>--startup-file no</code> or the number of <code>-p</code> or <code>--procs</code>, is no script and no code. The note names <code>mcp__kaimon__ex</code>, <code>ping</code> and <code>start_session</code>, and the keys of the live Kaimon sessions, which the Kaimon check reads from the same gate directory. It has three lines, and every matching call gets it.</p><p>The hook only adds a note: it refuses no call and asks nothing, so a probe that has a reason to run fresh, on a base, a head or a scratch environment, runs as it is. It leaves alone a call that names <code>julia</code> only as an argument or in a string, and a <code>julia</code> command with a <code>+&lt;version&gt;</code>, <code>--version</code> or <code>-v</code>, a script of the harness or of <code>.githooks/</code>, a <code>runtests.jl</code>, a <code>make.jl</code> or a script whose name ends in <code>-test.jl</code>, code that names <code>Pkg.</code> or a timing such as <code>@time</code> or <code>@btime</code>, and a command whose program is another one, such as <code>time</code>. It reads the command only and opens no script. It exits with status 0 for each input, and prints nothing when it cannot read the input. Test it:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;tool_name&quot;:&quot;Bash&quot;,&quot;tool_input&quot;:{&quot;command&quot;:&quot;julia -e 1&quot;}}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ./kaimon-note.py</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The install gives this hook to Claude Code only. OpenCode, oh-my-pi and Antigravity show no such note: their guard answers a call with a decision and a reason only, and has no channel for text on a call that runs.</p><h2 id="context-handoff-py" tabindex="-1"><code>context-handoff.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#context-handoff-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`context-handoff.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook tells a main session that its context has passed 400k tokens, and that the session hands off. Each model call reads the whole context again, so a long session pays for its size on every call. Claude Code runs the hook on <code>UserPromptSubmit</code> and, for every tool, on <code>PostToolUse</code>.</p><p>The hook reads the size from the transcript that the event names: the input, cache-write and cache-read tokens of the last model call of the main chain. It reads the transcript from its end and stops at the second model call, so a transcript of 50 MB costs no more than a small one. A run takes about 0.1 s, nearly all of it the start of Python.</p><p>At 400k tokens or more, the hook adds one line to the session&#39;s context. The line gives the size and tells the session to finish the current step, write the state of the work into the task file or a hand-off note, propose a new session with a short brief, and stop. On <code>UserPromptSubmit</code> the line comes with every prompt. On <code>PostToolUse</code> it comes only after the model call that passes 400k, 500k, 600k and so on, so a long turn gets it once per 100k tokens, with each tool that this one model call runs. A call of a sub-agent gets no line: its caller limits its context. A record that Claude Code writes itself, such as an API error, has the model <code>&lt;synthetic&gt;</code> and counts as no model call.</p><p>It blocks nothing and exits with status 0 for each input, also when it cannot read the input or the transcript. Test it:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;hook_event_name&quot;:&quot;UserPromptSubmit&quot;,&quot;transcript_path&quot;:&quot;&#39;&quot;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;">$TMPDIR</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">&quot;&#39;/t.jsonl&quot;}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ./context-handoff.py</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The install gives this hook to Claude Code only. Antigravity&#39;s <code>PreToolUse</code> payload holds the call and the workspace paths, and no transcript; the guard extension of oh-my-pi and the plugins of OpenCode do not read the context size.</p><h2 id="registry-copies-py" tabindex="-1"><code>registry-copies.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#registry-copies-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`registry-copies.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This hook warns after a <code>Bash</code> call that runs Julia when the depot holds a stale copy of the General registry. Every Julia version on a machine shares one depot, and the key <code>path</code> of <code>registries/General.toml</code> names the file of the registry that Pkg reads. Pkg of Julia 1.10 to 1.12 writes <code>General.tar.gz</code>, Pkg of Julia 1.13 writes <code>General.tar.zst</code>, and neither removes the file of the other. A read of the file that <code>General.toml</code> does not name then disagrees with what Pkg resolves. Claude Code runs the hook on <code>PostToolUse</code> for <code>Bash</code>.</p><p>The hook acts on a call with a simple command whose program is <code>julia</code> or <code>juliaup</code>, after the reserved words and the environment assignments. It reads <code>registries/</code> of the first entry of <code>JULIA_DEPOT_PATH</code>, or of <code>~/.julia</code>. When that directory holds more than one <code>General.tar.*</code> file, or when the file that <code>path</code> names is not the newest of them, it adds a warning to the session&#39;s context. The warning names each file with its modification time and the file that <code>General.toml</code> names. It gives the remedy: run <code>Pkg.Registry.update()</code> on the default Julia, then delete the copy that <code>General.toml</code> does not name.</p><p>It blocks nothing, edits nothing, and exits with status 0 for each input. It prints nothing when it cannot read the input, when the call runs no Julia, and when the directory or <code>General.toml</code> is missing. Test it:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#005CC5;--shiki-dark:#79B8FF;">echo</span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;"> &#39;{&quot;tool_name&quot;:&quot;Bash&quot;,&quot;tool_input&quot;:{&quot;command&quot;:&quot;julia -e 1&quot;}}&#39;</span><span style="--shiki-light:#D73A49;--shiki-dark:#F97583;"> |</span><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;"> ./registry-copies.py</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The install gives this hook to Claude Code only.</p><h2 id="probe-py" tabindex="-1"><code>probe.py</code> <a class="header-anchor" href="#probe-py" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;`probe.py`&quot;">​</a></h2><p>This script holds the test cases of the hooks and runs them. Its <code>GROUPS</code> split the cases into nine groups, and <code>harness test</code> runs each group as the part <code>probe:&lt;group&gt;</code>, so the probe needs no run of its own; <a href="./../development#tests">Development</a> describes the run.</p><p>A case is a hook, an input and the expected result. The probe runs each case in its own process, with a timeout, and prints one line per case: <code>ok</code> or <code>BAD</code>, the expected and the actual result. The last line gives the number of cases and the number of failed cases, and the exit status is 1 when any case fails. The group <code>commands</code> runs the guard package on each record of <code>tests/guard/corpus.jsonl</code> and on the cases that a line of it cannot hold: a command of 1 MiB and one byte more, commands of 1 MiB that the checks walk word by word, each held to 10 s with its seconds on its line, the count of the file-system reads, walk steps and <code>git</code> processes of such commands, a write target with a NUL, a <code>.git</code> that is a dangling link, a link to itself, a payload that is not UTF-8, the reason of the winning check, a frontend that the guard lacks, a guard that raises, the copy of <code>jsonio.py</code>, the file-system reads outside <code>paths.py</code>, and the rendered <code>Bash</code> entry with a guard that cannot start. The groups <code>omp</code> and <code>opencode</code> run each oh-my-pi or OpenCode record of the corpus through the extension or the plugin and the guard package, and the cases of the transport that a record cannot hold: an interpreter that is missing, a guard that exits 3, prints <code>{</code>, is killed or answers late, a path list that is missing, malformed or without <code>python</code>, and a message that the guard cannot read. The group <code>antigravity</code> runs the guard package with <code>--from antigravity</code> from a fixture home, on each Antigravity record of the corpus and on the cases that a record cannot hold: a broken path list, a payload that cannot be read, a call with no workspace, commands of many words and of 1 MiB with their seconds, the count of the reads and spawns of commands of 1 MiB (<code>[agy-work]</code>), the count of the matcher calls of the Bash rules on a crafted command of 1 MiB (<code>[mib-rules]</code>), the rendered handler, and a check that raises. The probe also covers the other Python hooks of this page, the fail-closed cases of <code>worktree.py</code>, and the drift cases of <code>install-drift.py</code>. It also loads <code>adapters/omp/guards.ts</code> and the OpenCode plugins under <code>node</code>, so <code>node</code> must be on <code>PATH</code>. The hooks run against a fixture <code>HOME</code> in a temporary directory, never the real one. With no argument it runs every group; with group names, only those groups. Each run builds its own fixture:</p><div class="language-bash vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang">bash</span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span style="--shiki-light:#6F42C1;--shiki-dark:#B392F0;">./probe.py</span><span style="--shiki-light:#24292E;--shiki-dark:#E1E4E8;"> [GROUP </span><span style="--shiki-light:#032F62;--shiki-dark:#9ECBFF;">...]</span></span></code></pre></div>',107)])])}const g=o(d,[["render",c]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
